    {
      "number": 1,
      "slug": "contains-duplicate",
      "title": "Contains Duplicate",
      "category": "Arrays & Hashing",
      "leetcode_number": 217,
      "neetcode_name": "Duplicate Integer",
      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "status": "Solved",
      "youtube_id": "3OamzN90kPg",
      "problem_statement": "Given an integer array <code>nums</code>, return <code>true</code> if any value appears at least twice in the array, and return <code>false</code> if every element is distinct.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "input": "nums = [1,2,3,1]",
          "output": "true"
        },
        {
          "input": "nums = [1,2,3,4]",
          "output": "false"
        },
        {
          "input": "nums = [1,1,1,3,3,4,3,2,4,2]",
          "output": "true"
        }
      ],
      "core_idea": {
        "goal": "Determine whether any value repeats in the array.",
        "key_insight": "A hash set gives O(1) average-case lookup and insertion, so a single pass can catch a repeat the moment it's seen, with no nested loops.",
        "trigger_phrase": "“Have I seen this value before?” → reach for a Hash Set membership check."
      },
      "optimal_solution": {
        "data_structure": "Hash Set (Python <code>set</code>)",
        "steps": [
          "Initialize an empty set <code>h</code>.",
          "Iterate through each <code>num</code> in <code>nums</code>.",
          "If <code>num</code> is already in <code>h</code>, return <code>True</code> immediately — duplicate found.",
          "Otherwise, add <code>num</code> to <code>h</code> and continue.",
          "If the loop finishes with no match, return <code>False</code>."
        ],
        "time_complexity": "O(n)",
        "space_complexity": "O(n)"
      },
      "code": "class Solution:\n    def hasDuplicate(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:\n        h = set()\n        for num in nums:\n            if num in h:\n                return True\n            else:\n                h.add(num)\n\n        return False",
      "walkthrough": [
        "Brute force compares every pair of elements, which costs O(n²) time. The moment we're willing to spend O(n) extra space, we can collapse that to a single O(n) pass.",
        "A Python <code>set</code> is backed by a hash table, so <code>num in h</code> and <code>h.add(num)</code> are both O(1) on average. We only need to know “have I seen this number before” — we never need to know where or how many times, so a set is a better fit than a dict or list.",
        "Walk the array once. For each number, check membership before inserting: if it's already there, you've found your duplicate and can short-circuit immediately rather than finishing the scan.",
        "If the loop completes with no early return, every element was unique, so the answer is <code>False</code>."
      ],
      "mental_shortcut": "“Seen it before? Set it and forget it.”",
      "related_problems": [
        "Contains Duplicate II (nearby-index variant)",
        "Contains Duplicate III (nearby-value variant)",
        "Longest Consecutive Sequence",
        "Single Number"
      ],
      "interview_talking_point": "I'm trading O(n) space for O(n) time with a hash set, which avoids the O(n²) brute-force approach of comparing every pair.",
      "edge_cases": [
        "Empty array → no duplicates possible, return False",
        "Single-element array → return False",
        "All elements identical → returns True as soon as the second element is checked",
        "Negative numbers and zero → ints are hashable as-is, no special handling needed"
      ],
      "trace": {
        "array": [
          1,
          2,
          3,
          1
        ],
